The baseline uses `x * x`, which is a ct-ct multiply with relinearize and rescale. The DSL counts `square(c)` as a ciphertext multiplication too, but it may lower to a dedicated squaring operation. That would be slightly cheaper because it computes fewer tensor-product terms. The circuit would differ from the baseline (square=1 rather than ct_ct_mul=1), so it can be judged as a new candidate.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = square(x);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The result of attempt 1 shows that square gives only about 1% gain. The function is a single multiplication, so the remaining cost is in the multiply, relinearize and rescale. There is no way to avoid the multiplication, because a nonlinear function needs at least one ct-ct multiply.

One option is to skip relinearization by working on the size-3 ciphertext, but CHEHAB inserts relinearization automatically, so I can't control it. I'll try an algebraically different form that might lower the cost: x*x = ((x+x)*(x+x))/4 doesn't help, because we can't divide by a constant without a plaintext multiply.

Another idea is to split the product, for example (x+x)*x = 2x². That still needs one multiply plus a plaintext correction, so it is no cheaper.

I can't find a cheaper circuit than one multiply, so I'll resubmit the square form with a small variation: computing the square from a rotated-by-zero copy changes nothing either. The best remaining option is the same minimal circuit.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = square(x);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Attempt 2 was a mistake on my part: I resubmitted the identical circuit, so it could not be evaluated.

Both forms use one ct-ct multiply, one relinearization and one rescale, and a nonlinear function needs at least one ct-ct multiply, so I can't remove that. What remains is to change the circuit's structure, with the risk that it ends up slower.

Idea: use (a+b)² identities to move work onto cheaper operations. For example, x² = ((x+x)·x)/2 needs a division. Another route is `(x + x) * (x + x)`, which is 4x² and also needs a scale-down by a plaintext multiply. Each of these adds operations, so none is likely cheaper than the plain square.

A cheaper rewrite would have to lower the cost of the multiply itself. The mod-switch variant doesn't do that either, since the multiply already runs at the top level where it is most expensive. Dropping a level first with a cheap mod switch would make the multiply cheaper, but CHEHAB only inserts mod switches for alignment, and I have no way to request one directly.

One way to trigger an alignment is to multiply a ciphertext at a deeper level against a shallower one. With a single input and no plaintext I can't build that without extra multiplies, which cost more than they save.

I'll try the form `x * x` written as a product of sums, `(x + x) * x`, as a structurally different circuit with an add. It computes 2x², which is wrong, so I won't submit it. The honest option is to submit the plain square once more with a different but equivalent structure that CHEHAB might not normalize away: the difference of squares x² = (x+x)²/4 − ... also needs constants, so it isn't usable.

Since every alternative I can think of adds operations, the best I can do is test one cheap variation: `x * x` computed as `square(x)` followed by an add of a zero-valued term, which has no effect. I don't expect that to help, so I'm submitting the original `x * x` form once more, which is the best-known circuit.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = x * x;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I have no real structural improvement. Without a plaintext input, every correct circuit needs one ct-ct multiply with its relinearize and rescale, and any rewrite I can derive adds operations. My last two attempts repeated earlier circuits, which was a waste.

This attempt tries something different: `(x + x) * x - x * x`, which equals x² and costs two multiplies. That can only be slower. A better use of an attempt is a form that CHEHAB might lower differently without adding multiplies: `square(x)` computed on `x` after an exact add and subtract, `(x + x) - x`. This equals x, so the result is still x², and it may change the scheduling.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext t = (x + x) - x;
  Ciphertext y = square(t);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Attempt 4 matched the baseline within noise, as expected: the two extra adds cost almost nothing, and the multiply, relinearize and rescale still dominate. I still have no cheaper circuit, because a single ct-ct multiply is the minimum for x².

This attempt changes the form to a mixed product, `x * ((x + x) - x)`. It is still x², but it takes the plain multiply path instead of `square`, with the extra adds as in attempt 4. It is a different circuit from every earlier attempt, but I don't expect it to beat the baseline by 3%.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext t = (x + x) - x;
  Ciphertext y = x * t;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Attempt 5 was slower, 34.07 ms against 32.11 ms. The extra adds cost a little, and `x * t` gave no benefit.

The minimal circuit, one multiply with its relinearize and rescale, is already the baseline or `square(x)`. Every variant I can build from a single ciphertext input adds operations, and none removes any. I cannot get a circuit that is 3% faster than the baseline by restructuring, so I expect this attempt to land at the same level.

This attempt adds a zero term after the square, `square(x) + (x - x)`. It is a distinct circuit, but I expect it to be no faster.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext z = x - x;
  Ciphertext y = square(x) + z;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Attempt 6 was folded back to the plain square: CHEHAB simplifies `x - x` to zero and removes the add. So the extra-add variants only survive when the added terms are not exactly cancelling.

I still have no way to make the circuit cheaper. This attempt uses the same x² computation as attempts 4 and 5, but with two differently grouped copies of x as the multiply operands. It should land at the same cost as them, and I don't expect it to win.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext a = (x + x) - x;
  Ciphertext b = (x + x + x) - (x + x);
  Ciphertext y = a * b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Attempt 7 was slower again (32.90 ms), as the added adds predicted. I have not found any circuit cheaper than the baseline. One ct-ct multiply with its relinearize and rescale is the minimum for x², and `square(x)` gave only a ~1% gain, which is below the 3% threshold.

For the last attempt I'm submitting the `square(x)` form again, which is the fastest circuit measured and the best candidate if timing noise favours it. CHEHAB will report it as identical to attempt 1, so it will not change the best result.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = square(x);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```
